agencyDescription:
The Ministry of Finance (MOF) is responsible for managing Singapore’s fiscal policies and the structure of its economy. We work to maintain international standards and best practices in areas such as company law, accounting standards, and corporate governance principles. We aim to advance the well-being and development of Singapore by:

•	achieving a balanced budget through prudent and sustainable fiscal policies;
•	ensuring the prudent investment of the government's reserves and other public funds;
•	setting out policies for government procurement, customs regulation, accounting standards and business regulation;
•	fostering a regulatory environment conducive to business and enterprise; and
•	ensuring that businesses operating within Singapore's jurisdiction are compatible with international standards and practices, in areas such as company law, accounting standards and corporate governance principles.

jobResponsibilities:
• Identify and study emerging trends and assess their impact on families;
• Conceptualise, implement and drive family-related research projects;
• Analyse and report research and evaluation findings as well as identify their implications for policy and programmes;
• Formulate and review policies with other agencies to address potential gaps to support various types of families;
• Support the strategic planning process for the Directorate; and
• Lead in the planning and organising of the biennial Asian Family Conference

As Deputy Director, you will lead a diverse and dedicated team in engaging our stakeholders in the South Asia, Middle East and Africa Markets. 
You will support your team to: 
•	Track and analyse key geostrategic and economic developments and trends in your markets and assess their impact on Singapore’s trade and investment interests; 
•	Develop, implement and review strategies, policies and initiatives that enhances Singapore’s bilateral and regional economic relations with countries in SAMEA;
•	Represent MTI during visits, meetings, negotiations and conferences, and where relevant, lead negotiations and discussions with international counterparts; 
•	Build networks and relationships with foreign stakeholders, including government agencies, private companies, business chambers, thinktanks and universities; and 
•	Drive effective talent management and staff capability development to meet the team’s current and future requirements.

•	Implement and strengthen the governance, systems and processes of records management (RM) across MOM;
•	Provide advisory and strong support to departments and teams in the capability development and implementation of records management;
•	Manage the assigned administrative functions and work areas for records management, review of SOPs, records appraisals, housekeeping and updating of file plan, etc.; 
•	Drive and implement key initiatives and projects for records management (RM), such as digitalisation of physical files and records, RM chatbot, etc.;
•	Work with IT team and departments on implementing key RM system features to support RM needs, integrating the RM lifecycle in the systems upgrading and renewal;
•	Plan, manage and coordinate various corporate administrative operations so as to ensure they are carried out effectively and efficiently;
•	Review, develop and implement improvements for work processes and systems related to corporate administration.

The study will see the interns engaged in the following: 

(i) Desktop research and analysis, such as on GIS-related datasets and case studies on relevant urban planning concepts (e.g., 15 minute city typologies);
(ii) Field research, such as site visits to assess the built-environment and streetscape conditions of shortlisted towns and neighbourhoods;
(iii) Planning and execution for inter-agency workshops centred around the research findings, including administrative work like invitations, scheduling, note taking, on-site management of workshop logistics etc.

Reporting to the General Counsel, you will assist to advise on, develop and implement regulatory policies and actions. You will provide legal advice and support to GRA in all aspects of the performance of its statutory functions and duties. You will be assigned to the following areas of work:
•	Policy: work with management to develop and implement regulatory policies;
•	Legislation: draft regulatory instruments such as subsidiary legislation, approvals, notices, directions and licenses;
•	Enforcement: provide advice on regulatory matters arising from the Gambling Control Act and Casino Control Act, the regulations thereunder, and other related laws;
•	Corporate: draft and vet contractual documents such as tenders, employment contracts, insurance policies and other commercial agreements;
•	Secretarial: provide secretariat support to GRA Board sub-committees;
•	Training: provide legal training to GRA officers;
•	Public Engagement: actively involved in engaging the public on GRA’s mandate.

•	Implement and strengthen the governance, systems and processes of records management (RM) across MOM;
